WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.920
Reboots, reimaginings, the odd&nbsp;
behind-the-scenes spat: Actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.480
can be replaced between movies for any number of&nbsp;
reasons — and sometimes it goes very, very wrong.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.000
In the early '90s, Michael Keaton&nbsp;
handed over the keys for Wayne Manor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.600
having portrayed Bruce Wayne in 1989's Batman&nbsp;
and its 1992 sequel Batman Returns. Reportedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.040
Keaton refused to reprise the role in the&nbsp;
third film after reading the screenplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.480
and in the years since has pointed the blame&nbsp;
for his departure squarely at the writers.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:36.240
Val Kilmer clearly didn't share Keaton's&nbsp;
misgivings. The Tombstone star accepted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.640
the role as-is and went into the&nbsp;
film knowing he'd have to contend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.401
with a famous cast of villains and a new director.

00:00:42.401 --> 00:00:50.440
"Riddle me this, riddle me that,&nbsp;
who's afraid of the big black bat?"

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:55.880
In the end, Joel Schumacher shouldered a&nbsp;
lot of the blame for 1995's Batman Forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.440
though his leading man didn't escape&nbsp;
criticism. Kilmer's performance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.960
described as "utterly lethargic" and&nbsp;
"god-awful," with a review published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.960
by Film4 specifically calling out Keaton's&nbsp;
replacement as being simply "not up to the job."

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.480
Many planned Superman projects failed to&nbsp;
come to fruition in the years after 1987's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:20.480
resoundingly terrible Superman IV: The&nbsp;
Quest For Peace. Nearly 20 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:26.240
the Man of Steel returned to the big screen for&nbsp;
2006's Superman Returns. Director Bryan Singer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.920
take on DC's prized property proved to be a solid&nbsp;
enough platform to take the franchise forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.680
though the vast budget meant that the movie&nbsp;
wasn't a financial success. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.640
Brandon Routh's turn as the Man&nbsp;
of Steel drew praise from many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.560
with Newsweek in particular gushing over Routh&nbsp;
for the way in which the newcomer "effortlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:50.640
lays claim to the iconic role." The outlet even&nbsp;
favorably compared him to Christopher Reeve.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.720
Although DC intended to produce a sequel to&nbsp;
Superman Returns, every year that passed made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.960
it less likely to happen. Despite Routh&nbsp;
making it quite clear that he wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:05.120
don the red cape again, the studio opted&nbsp;
to reboot the franchise with Man of Steel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.800
a Zack Snyder reboot fronted by Henry Cavill.&nbsp;
Trouble is, Snyder's film took the aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.600
Superman Returns that people didn't like —&nbsp;
the morose, humorless tone — and amplified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.000
it. Cavill's downbeat portrayal of the&nbsp;
titular hero has been criticized for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.960
being too brooding and too dark, leading to a&nbsp;
Superman film that's practically devoid of joy.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.280
Today, Back to the Future stands as&nbsp;
a timeless classic that continues to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:35.720
delight audiences of all ages. Crispin Glover&nbsp;
played the role of Marty's father, George,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:41.640
in arguably his most iconic role. From cowardly&nbsp;
creep to confident ladies' man, George undergoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.120
the most significant transformation in&nbsp;
the trilogy — both on and off camera.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.360
In fact, some might not even realize that Glover&nbsp;
didn't return for Back to the Future Part II, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.120
which he was replaced by Jeffrey Weissman wearing&nbsp;
Glover-inspired prosthetics. Despite having fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.600
recast Jennifer Parker for the sequels, the&nbsp;
filmmakers opted to hide their new George McFly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:08.000
via prosthetics and clever filmmaking techniques&nbsp;
that made it nearly impossible to spot the fake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.680
Originally, co-writer and co-producer Bob Gale&nbsp;
claimed that Glover quit due to a pay dispute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.200
but Glover himself has denied this on a number&nbsp;
of occasions. Instead, he says, he was removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.640
from the burgeoning franchise after voicing his&nbsp;
distaste for the ending of the original movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.800
which he believed implied that the McFlys'&nbsp;
happiness was dependent on material wealth.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.360
"Woah, this is heavy."

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.360
For Part II, the filmmakers spliced footage&nbsp;
of Glover's performance from the first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:42.400
added new material with Weissman, and killed&nbsp;
off George McFly in the "alternate 1985" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.800
keep audiences from discovering&nbsp;
the truth. It worked well enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.920
but upon rewatch it's clear how hard&nbsp;
director Robert Zemeckis tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.480
hide the fact that Glover wasn't involved.&nbsp;
Perhaps unsurprisingly, Glover was less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.200
thrilled about his face being used without his&nbsp;
permission. Years later, he told the AV Club:

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.680
"They thought that was funny. They knew that they&nbsp;
could basically torment me, either financially or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:10.000
by [...] what ultimately was an illegal thing to&nbsp;
do. I’m sure they laughed and joked about it."

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.720
Live-action Bruce Waynes tend to find a new&nbsp;
love interest in each installment of the Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:20.160
franchise. From Kim Basinger's Vicki Vale to&nbsp;
Nicole Kidman's Dr. Chase Meridian, the Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.880
Knight certainly gets around. But for Christopher&nbsp;
Nolan's visionary take on the character's origins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:31.040
a new bat-love was born. In 2005's Batman&nbsp;
Begins, Katie Holmes played Bruce Wayne's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.040
childhood-friend-turned-love-interest&nbsp;
Rachel Dawes.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:39.040
At the end of the film, Rachel tells Bruce that&nbsp;
they can only be together when Gotham City no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.360
longer needs Batman, setting up an intriguing&nbsp;
arc for the two characters. However, Holmes quit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:50.400
Nolan's Batman sequel in favor of the buddy comedy&nbsp;
Mad Money; she was replaced by Maggie Gyllenhaal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.000
in The Dark Knight. No doubt, Gyllenhaal delivers&nbsp;
an excellent performance, and she certainly fits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.080
the tone of the sequel, but it's hard to believe&nbsp;
that this Rachel is the same character as Holmes'.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:04.080
Not only is Gyllenhaal's Rachel more&nbsp;
cavalier than her Begins counterpart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.120
but she doesn't seem as personally&nbsp;
invested in Bruce as she did before. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:12.640
some of that is due to plot reasons — namely,&nbsp;
her romance with Harvey Dent — but it also may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.960
be that Gyllenhaal simply didn't have the same&nbsp;
chemistry with Christian Bale as her predecessor.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:22.920
Hugo Weaving's absence was sorely felt in&nbsp;
The Matrix Resurrections. That's not to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.400
that Jonathan Groff, who replaced Weaving&nbsp;
as the villainous program known as Smith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.680
is not a talented actor. Sadly, he had huge&nbsp;
shoes to fill: Even in Reloaded and Revolutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.320
Weaving shines brightest among the cast, while&nbsp;
Ian Bliss tackles an inspired impersonation when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.273
the character takes over a human host.&nbsp;
But the fourth time was not the charm.

00:05:43.273 --> 00:05:47.553
"Mr. Anderson!"

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:48.920
"No…"

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:53.280
Considering Weaving's iconic turn as Smith,&nbsp;
a new version of the character needed to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.880
either identical or significantly different from&nbsp;
the original in order to work. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:03.400
Groff's portrayal in Resurrections is neither.&nbsp;
Deemed "an algorithm of unoriginality" by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:07.480
Guardian, the fourth Matrix movie failed&nbsp;
to recapture what made the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:12.960
between Neo and Smith so special, and that's&nbsp;
due at least in part to Weaving's absence.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.560
There was plenty of controversy surrounding&nbsp;
the making of the animated movie Scoob!,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:22.000
a reboot feature meant to kickstart a new&nbsp;
theatrical Scooby-Doo franchise. Between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.240
unceremoniously canceled sequels and the film's&nbsp;
failed attempt to launch a cinematic universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.400
the biggest problem with Scoob! was its&nbsp;
casting. Despite retaining Frank Welker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:35.640
as the iconic voice of Scooby-Doo, the&nbsp;
rest of the Mystery Inc. gang was scrapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:39.520
for this installment, including&nbsp;
Matthew Lillard's Shaggy Rogers.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:45.000
Lillard garnered widespread acclaim when he played&nbsp;
Shaggy in the two live-action Scooby-Doo movies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.480
the early 2000s; he went on to take over vocal&nbsp;
duties from Casey Kasem for subsequent animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:56.000
movies and television productions. To this day,&nbsp;
Lillard still plays Shaggy as faithfully as ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.920
but he missed out on the 2020 return to the&nbsp;
big screen. After the film's cast was revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.320
Lillard took to X, formerly&nbsp;
known as Twitter, to write:

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.080
"Well this sucks. What a crappy way&nbsp;
to find out…. thanks Hollywood."

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.520
Of course, Lillard wasn't the only&nbsp;
one replaced in the new movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.320
nor was he the only one disappointed by the&nbsp;
news — but, given his many years playing Shaggy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.280
it was tough news nonetheless. And it&nbsp;
didn't help that Will Forte's Shaggy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:24.360
just sounded like a higher-pitched Will&nbsp;
Forte rather than Shaggy. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:29.440
Lillard has since resumed voicing&nbsp;
Shaggy in all other Scooby-Doo projects.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.520
Some movies should never be remade. We don't need&nbsp;
another Citizen Kane, Casablanca, or Vertigo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.320
for example, and we certainly didn't need another&nbsp;
Psycho. What's worse is that director Gus Van Sant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.040
chose to remake the film nearly shot-for-shot,&nbsp;
even copying some of Alfred Hitchcock's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.520
signature camera movements. It's unsettling&nbsp;
to be sure — but maybe that was the point.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.600
In any case, Anthony Perkins, who gave a&nbsp;
straight-up iconic performance in the 1960&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:59.640
original, can't be easily overshadowed.&nbsp;
So, when Vince Vaughn was cast as Norman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:04.960
Bates in the remake, folks were understandably&nbsp;
skeptical. Given Vaughn's usual filmography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.680
his casting in Psycho made for something&nbsp;
a strange choice. As Roger Ebert wrote:

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:13.240
"He isn't odd enough. Norman's&nbsp;
early dialogue often ends in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.720
a nervous laugh. [...] Vaughn's&nbsp;
laugh doesn't seem involuntary."

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:21.400
When all is said and done, Vaughn's Norman is&nbsp;
still a psycho, but he's a strange and unlayered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:25.920
one at best, and not nearly as frightening as&nbsp;
Perkins' original. Like the rest of Van Sant's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:31.280
Psycho remake, Vaughn's performance proves&nbsp;
the folly in trying to reinvent the wheel.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.920
Can you believe that there have been no fewer than&nbsp;
six Home Alone movies? Obviously, the first two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:42.120
directed by Chris Columbus and starring Macaulay&nbsp;
Culkin, are the best by far. Over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.360
Home Alone and Lost In New York&nbsp;
have become Christmas classics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.840
while the other less-inventive sequels&nbsp;
simply rode the franchise's coattails.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.960
"You guys give up, or are you thirsty for more?"

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:56.960
But though most of the sequels feature a&nbsp;
new protagonist in the place of young Kevin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:01.640
the direct-to-video Home Alone 4&nbsp;
opted to resurrect the original lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:03.600
albeit without Culkin's involvement.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.480
The fourth film takes place seemingly&nbsp;
a few years after Lost in New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:12.600
with the McCallister family in complete disarray&nbsp;
— and not just because they've all been recast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:18.240
too. Here, Kevin is played by Mike Weinberg, who&nbsp;
isn't nearly as charismatic or lovable as Culkin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:22.480
though he does look younger. In fact, if the&nbsp;
character's name had been changed, you'd have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:27.040
clue that this was supposed to be the same kid.&nbsp;
No doubt, Culkin was looked over because Home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:33.120
Alone 4 released 12 years after the original, but&nbsp;
that's no excuse. As the Radio Times review says:

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:34.720
"You have to wonder why they bothered."

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.440
Not only was Home Alone 4 destined to be a&nbsp;
train wreck after a poor third installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:44.360
but trying to capitalize on the McCallister&nbsp;
family without utilizing any returning faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.800
turned out to be nothing short of a disaster.&nbsp;
Let's chalk it all up to real Kevin's bad dream.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:56.760
Billy Dee Williams played a pre-disfigured version&nbsp;
of Harvey Dent in Tim Burton's 1989 Batman. Dent's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.240
appearance was no doubt meant to tease the&nbsp;
character's on-screen future as Two-Face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:06.680
a turn he finally made in Batman Forever. But in&nbsp;
that film, it wasn't Williams facing off against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:12.400
the Dark Knight, but Tommy Lee Jones. We all know&nbsp;
that Val Kilmer replaced Michael Keaton as Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:18.200
but many forget that Williams' Dent and Jones'&nbsp;
Two-Face are meant to be one and the same.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:26.800
"Very few people are both a summer and&nbsp;
a winter, but you pull it off nicely."

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:31.200
Of course, there's a clear difference between the&nbsp;
two iterations of the character. Appearance aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:36.080
Williams' Dent is a charismatic leader, hoping to&nbsp;
use his status to change the hearts and minds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:41.120
voters. On the contrary, Jones' Two-Face&nbsp;
isn't much more than a maniacal sidekick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.880
to Jim Carrey's over-the-top Riddler, which&nbsp;
only makes Batman Forever feel all the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:50.960
outlandish. Roger Ebert may have once deemed&nbsp;
Forever better than Batman Returns, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:56.240
movie's strange tone and misuse of Tommy Lee Jones&nbsp;
make it a controversial bat-flick to this day.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:02.720
In 2021, DC Comics published a comic continuation&nbsp;
to Batman and Batman Returns that follows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.360
Michael Keaton incarnation of the Dark Knight.&nbsp;
Written by Batman screenwriter Sam Hamm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:11.880
the series revives Williams' version of Harvey&nbsp;
Dent and chronicles the character's troubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:17.120
turn into Two-Face, thus establishing the&nbsp;
events of Forever as an alternate timeline.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.560
Tom Cruise brought the vampire Lestat de&nbsp;
Lioncourt to life in the film adaptation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:27.320
Anne Rice's novel Interview with the Vampire.&nbsp;
Cruise's memorable performance opposite Brad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:31.480
Pitt and a young Kirsten Dunst is certainly&nbsp;
a highlight of the film — but did you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:36.560
there was a sequel? Cruise didn't reprise his&nbsp;
vampiric role, however, which is probably why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:42.480
many people haven't heard of it. Instead, Stuart&nbsp;
Townsend took over for 2002's Queen of the Damned.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:47.720
Also based on an Anne Rice novel, Queen of the&nbsp;
Damned was a complete and utter failure. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:52.520
did the movie tank at the box office, but Rice&nbsp;
wasn't particularly thrilled with it either, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:57.600
no other movies based on The Vampire Chronicles&nbsp;
have been released since. While Townsend may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:01.840
have better resembled the character from the&nbsp;
novel in terms of physicality, he didn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:06.960
the same sort of gravitas that Cruise brought to&nbsp;
the role. Film Threat's review of the movie read:

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:11.960
"Tom Cruise had the good sense to stay away&nbsp;
from reprising his role of Lestat in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:17.320
film. And audiences should demand this film be&nbsp;
buried never to see the light of day again."

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:22.120
Hugo Weaving wasn't the only actor replaced in&nbsp;
The Matrix Resurrections. Laurence Fishburne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:26.480
played Morpheus in all three installments&nbsp;
of the original trilogy as well as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:30.680
in-canon Matrix Online video game, which&nbsp;
chronicled the character's death. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:34.360
of his work in The Matrix, Fishburne&nbsp;
has become a Hollywood staple; he even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:39.720
reunited with his former co-star Keanu Reeves&nbsp;
on multiple occasions in the John Wick series.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.000
"At last."

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:45.200
Rather than keep Morpheus in the grave,&nbsp;
The Matrix Resurrections resurrects the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:48.935
character, now played by Yahya Abdul-Mateen II.

00:12:48.935 --> 00:12:57.360
"At last… Ah, wasn't too sure&nbsp;
about the callback. But, you know…"

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:03.320
Much like Agent Smith's recasting, Abdul-Mateen&nbsp;
makes a valiant attempt to bring Morpheus back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:08.800
life. This Morpheus isn't the original, but rather&nbsp;
a program meant to help Neo achieve his destiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:12.680
which makes this red pill a little&nbsp;
easier to swallow. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:17.640
the lack of Fishburne no doubt contributed to the&nbsp;
critical and commercial failures of Resurrections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:21.840
Every Matrix sequel has proved that it's&nbsp;
nearly impossible to recapture what made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:27.480
the original great — but at least the first two&nbsp;
follow-ups retained most of their original casts.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:31.960
Compared to the first two James Cameron&nbsp;
films, it's understandable why most folks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:37.280
dislike Terminator 3: Rise of the Machines. The&nbsp;
movie tries a bit too hard with the jokes and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:42.520
aside from John Connor's compelling character arc,&nbsp;
it doesn't really break any new ground. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:48.200
one of the strongest aspects of T3 is the budding&nbsp;
romance between John and a young Kate Brewster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:54.280
who learns all about Skynet, Terminators, and&nbsp;
Judgment Day just a day or so before it occurs.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:14:00.280
"The end of the world… it's&nbsp;
today, three hours from now."&nbsp;

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.240
"Two hours and fifty-three minutes."

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:05.320
So when the movie ends and John&nbsp;
and Kate are trapped underground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.320
we're left to wonder… What's next?

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:11.920
Half a decade later, Terminator: Salvation&nbsp;
replaced Nick Stahl's John Connor with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:17.080
Christian Bale and Claire Danes' Kate Brewster&nbsp;
with Bryce Dallas Howard. Compared to Danes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:22.120
Howard's portrayal is fairly one-note, though, to&nbsp;
be fair, she isn't given half as much to do as her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:27.760
predecessor. Salvation barely even centers around&nbsp;
John Connor, let alone Kate, so it's no surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:33.040
that Howard had little to work with. In a 2009&nbsp;
interview with CinemaBlend, the actor shared:

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:36.320
"The way that my character best&nbsp;
serviced the film was being kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:39.520
of an emotional sounding board for John Connor."

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:45.040
While the Mortal Kombat video games and animated&nbsp;
movies have done particularly well over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:49.680
translating such a stylized world into&nbsp;
live-action can prove difficult. So when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:55.880
director Paul W.S. Anderson released his vision&nbsp;
in 1995, fans were surprisingly happy with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.880 --> 00:15:01.080
results. The film starred Christopher Lambert as&nbsp;
Raiden, along with an ensemble cast that did a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:06.280
decent job at bringing these characters to life.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, he didn't return in the sequel.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:10.120
Years later, Lambert revealed that he&nbsp;
didn't like the script for Mortal Kombat:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:14.480
Annihilation — and who can blame him?&nbsp;
Taking over as Raiden was James Remar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:18.760
who wasn't exactly notable in the role.&nbsp;
Although IGN later admitted that Remar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:23.760
played the part well, the outlet couldn't get&nbsp;
past how bad the rest of the movie was, saying:

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.840
"Hilariously awful matte shots, cheap&nbsp;
costumes, obvious sets and some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:34.560
worst CG ever put in a mainstream release&nbsp;
fill nearly every scene of this movie."

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:40.400
No doubt, Mortal Kombat: Annihilation is a total&nbsp;
train wreck, and, even though James Remar did his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:45.840
best to make his part memorable, it only reminds&nbsp;
us of how terrible the rest of the movie is.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:50.920
Halloweentown was one of the most beloved Disney&nbsp;
Channel features to hit TV screens. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:55.440
it was so popular with '90s kids that two&nbsp;
sequels were made in the years following. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.440 --> 00:16:01.400
then a darker installment came, following Marnie&nbsp;
Piper into her college years. Only this time, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:07.520
wasn't played by the lovable Kimberly J. Brown,&nbsp;
as Sara Paxton instead stepped into the role.

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:12.440
Return to Halloweentown was kind of a mess.&nbsp;
Though the majority of the original cast returned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:17.920
Paxton was the odd one out, playing an older&nbsp;
Marnie to whom viewers didn't recognize or relate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:22.360
Strangely, some House of Mouse witchcraft&nbsp;
must have been afoot, since there are two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:29.400
conflicting accounts of why Brown was recast in&nbsp;
the first place. In 2010, Brown told Oct31st.org:

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:33.960
"I wanted to do the fourth one and was fully&nbsp;
available for the shoot. Disney decided not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:38.600
use me for the fourth one, [I] don't know why.&nbsp;
I was definitely disappointed not to do it."

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:43.840
But producer Sheri Singer had a different&nbsp;
story. In 2017, she told E! Online:

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:45.960
"It was not something we wanted to do. We couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:50.271
make the deal work. That was why&nbsp;
and we didn't want to not do it."

00:16:50.271 --> 00:16:54.853
Sometimes, movies reference the fact&nbsp;
that a certain actor has been recast.

00:16:54.853 --> 00:16:58.600
"Hey buddy, didn't expect to see you here."&nbsp;

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:02.000
"Look, it's me, I'm here,&nbsp;
deal with it. Let's move on."

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.920
Occasionally, a movie even takes&nbsp;
pains to explain the change in actor;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:10.880
It could have been a dream, plastic surgery, or&nbsp;
some other strange off-screen development. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:14.880
every now and again, they just straight-up&nbsp;
admit they couldn't afford the real deal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:18.200
That was the case with George of&nbsp;
the Jungle 2. In the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:23.440
George was played by the ever-talented Brendan&nbsp;
Fraser. But, for Disney's direct-to-video sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:28.800
Christopher Showerman took over the titular role.&nbsp;
And the filmmakers weren't shy about admitting it:

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:32.400
"Me new George. Studio too&nbsp;
cheap to pay Brendan Fraser."

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:36.080
Of course, George isn't the only&nbsp;
character recast in the sequel:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:41.040
Julie Benz replaced Leslie Mann as&nbsp;
Ursula Stanhope as well. Strangely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:45.720
Thomas Haden Church reprised his role as&nbsp;
Lyle Van de Groot from the first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:50.600
though even his presence couldn't save this one.&nbsp;
If the direct-to-video budget didn't give it away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:56.880
George of the Jungle 2 is full of bad CGI, a&nbsp;
retread of the first film's plot, and, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:02.960
absolutely no Brendan Fraser in sight. In a review&nbsp;
from DVDVerdict, Showerman was described as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:09.880
"non-entity" who only serves as an "imitation&nbsp;
of Brendan Fraser's interpretation of George."

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:15.560
Nobody took over Keanu Reeves' role as Officer&nbsp;
Jack Traven from the '90s action classic Speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:21.360
but The Lost Boys legend Jason Patric replaced&nbsp;
him as the male lead for its sequel, Speed 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:25.360
Cruise Control. Reeves instead&nbsp;
chose to pursue other action roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:30.480
which eventually led to the likes of Constantine&nbsp;
and John Wick. But with Reeves out for the count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:36.200
and Patric stepping in as Officer Alex&nbsp;
Shaw, Speed 2 could only end in a wreck.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:40.120
And wreck it did. Many cited Patric's&nbsp;
performance and the screenplay as some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:43.520
of the film's biggest issues.&nbsp;
TIME magazine's review said:

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:46.960
"He's stalwart and athletic, but&nbsp;
fundamentally uninteresting."

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:51.640
Others, including original Speed star Sandra&nbsp;
Bullock, wished they could forget about Cruise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:56.440
Control entirely. Interestingly, Reeves&nbsp;
is apparently open to returning as Jack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.440 --> 00:19:01.520
Traven one day. While promoting John Wick:&nbsp;
Chapter 4, Reeves told Access Hollywood that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:06.440
a "great story and a great script" would&nbsp;
convince him to come back for Speed 3.

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:12.000
After the commercial success of the 1994&nbsp;
live-action adaptation of The Flintstones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:17.880
a sequel was certainly on the cards at Universal&nbsp;
Pictures. So when another live-action Flintstones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:22.400
titled The Flintstones in Viva Rock Vegas,&nbsp;
showed up in theaters six years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:26.600
nobody was particularly surprised. The&nbsp;
first film had been panned by critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:30.860
but the second — well, that was a real disaster.

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:32.840
"Yabba-dabba-doo!"

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:37.160
For starters, Viva Rock Vegas is&nbsp;
a prequel rather than a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:42.160
and the movie recasts its comedic leads. The&nbsp;
original movie starred John Goodman as Fred,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:47.680
Rick Moranis as Barney, Elizabeth Perkins&nbsp;
as Wilma, and Rosie O'Donnell as Betty. Viva&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:54.000
Rock Vegas starred Mark Addy as Fred, Stephen&nbsp;
Baldwin as Barney, Kristen Johnson as Wilma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:58.880
and Jane Krakowski as Betty. To say that any&nbsp;
of these actors are untalented on their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:02.800
would be untrue, but that doesn't mean&nbsp;
they were the right folks for the job.

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:08.560
While The Flintstones was no masterpiece itself,&nbsp;
the lack of comedic timing, uninspired jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:13.560
and lackluster cast of the sequel hardly&nbsp;
set it up for success. As Roger Ebert wrote:

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:19.200
"Minute by weary minute the movie wends its&nbsp;
weary way toward its joyless conclusion."

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:23.640
No wonder a third live-action&nbsp;
Flintstones has never materialized.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:28.880
One of the few things that Terminator Genisys did&nbsp;
well was recreate the opening sequence from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:33.680
original James Cameron movie. From the de-aged&nbsp;
Arnold Schwarzenegger to the rebuilt sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:36.920
the 1984 sequence is an exquisite duplication that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:41.200
totally holds up to the original. Of&nbsp;
course, Genisys diverges from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:45.640
with the biggest change being the lack&nbsp;
of '80s action icon Michael Biehn.

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:47.440
"Come with me if you wanna live."

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:53.080
While Emilia Clarke sort of resembles Linda&nbsp;
Hamilton's Sarah Connor, Jai Courtney looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:58.600
absolutely nothing like the Kyle Reese we're used&nbsp;
to. To its credit, 2009's Terminator Salvation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:05.240
featured a younger version of the character, one&nbsp;
that still embodied the Kyle we all know and love.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:06.920
"Come with me if you wanna live."

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:11.080
But Courtney, who lacks a certain&nbsp;
kind of charisma, doesn't translate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:15.760
well at all. Unfortunately, Genisys was&nbsp;
already a mess and a half to begin with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:20.160
and a planned series of movies was quickly&nbsp;
canceled after the movie faced an increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:25.880
negative reception. Unsurprisingly, Kyle Reese&nbsp;
hasn't appeared in a Terminator movie since.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:31.400
In the original X-Men films, team leader Scott&nbsp;
Summers was played by James Marsden. Though he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:36.160
written a bit too much like a jealous boyfriend&nbsp;
at times, Marsden did a decent job bringing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:41.280
Marvel hero to life, especially when it came to&nbsp;
his dynamic with Hugh Jackman's Wolverine. But by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:46.880
the time X-Men: The Last Stand came around, the&nbsp;
actor had decided to star in Superman Returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:52.480
forcing Cyclops' untimely demise. Thankfully,&nbsp;
once Wolverine fixed the timeline in X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:57.200
Days of Future Past, Scott was resurrected,&nbsp;
giving us a new hope for his future.

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:02.360
And then he was recast. X-Men: Apocalypse,&nbsp;
the direct sequel to First Class and Days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:07.120
of Future Past, introduced audiences to&nbsp;
a younger Scott Summers, one who has yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:12.760
to grow into the hero embodied by Marsden in&nbsp;
the original trilogy. Played by Tye Sheridan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:15.480
Cyclops is no longer the&nbsp;
field leader of the X-Men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:21.440
nor does he have much agency in a movie that&nbsp;
mostly centers on Xavier, Magneto, and Mystique.

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:26.480
Despite Sheridan's struggle to bring Cyclops to&nbsp;
life in Apocalypse and its sequel, Dark Phoenix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.240
James Marsden supported his successor, citing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:34.400
young actor's role in Mud as a particular&nbsp;
accomplishment. In 2015, he told HitFix:

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:37.960
"I think he's a wonderfully talented&nbsp;
actor. I think he's a perfect fit."

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:42.680
Few actors perfectly embody their comic book&nbsp;
counterparts quite like the cast of the Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:48.840
Cinematic Universe's Phase One. Robert Downey,&nbsp;
Jr. as Iron Man, Chris Evans as Captain America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:55.440
and Samuel L. Jackson as Nick Fury were certainly&nbsp;
inspired, as was Edward Norton as Bruce Banner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.440 --> 00:23:01.520
aka the Incredible Hulk. Appearing as the angry&nbsp;
green giant in 2008's The Incredible Hulk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:06.840
Norton's portrayal of the runaway scientist felt&nbsp;
ripped from the original Marvel Comics themselves.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:12.160
Not only was Norton able to deftly balance Bruce's&nbsp;
struggles with mental health and his desire for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:18.040
impossible intimacy, but his Hulk was also fierce,&nbsp;
powerful, and best embodied the character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:24.400
rage. But then Norton exited the MCU, never to&nbsp;
return. In 2014, he told the Hollywood Reporter:

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:29.080
"My feeling was that I experimented and&nbsp;
experienced what I wanted to. I really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:32.760
really enjoyed it. And yet, I looked&nbsp;
at the balance of time in life that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:38.000
one spends not only making those sorts of&nbsp;
films but then especially putting them out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:40.200
and the obligations that rightly come with that."

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:47.720
So, for 2012's grand team-up feature The Avengers,&nbsp;
Marvel recast Mark Ruffalo as Bruce Banner.

00:23:47.720 --> 00:23:53.080
"That's my secret, Cap. I'm always angry."

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:58.040
Ruffalo has played the reluctant hero ever&nbsp;
since, but unfortunately, his more comedic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:02.760
take on the character feels too far removed&nbsp;
from Norton's intricately complex dive into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:08.637
Bruce Banner's psyche. In this case, at least, it&nbsp;
seems like the MCU opted for style over substance.

